Now I understand the structure. The `substr_offsets` array has `substr_count+1` elements (with the last one being the final offset == raw_size). The `substr_types` array has `substr_count` elements.

In `render_attribute`:
```c
for(i = 0; attr->substr_offsets[i] < attr->size; i++) {
    MD_TEXTTYPE type = attr->substr_types[i];
    MD_OFFSET off = attr->substr_offsets[i];
    MD_SIZE size = attr->substr_offsets[i+1] - off;
```

The loop iterates while `substr_offsets[i] < attr->size`. The last offset `substr_offsets[substr_count]` equals `attr->size` (which is `off`). So the loop should stop at `i = substr_count`. But wait - there's a subtle issue. Let me think about this.

Actually, let me look at the `render_attribute` function more carefully. The issue is that `attr->size` is set to `off` in `md_build_attribute`, and `substr_offsets[substr_count] = off`. So the loop condition `substr_offsets[i] < attr->size` will be true for `i < substr_count` and false at `i = substr_count`. That seems correct.

But wait - what if `attr->size` is 0? Let me check the trivial case. If `raw_size == 0`, then `build->text = NULL`, `substr_count = 1`, `trivial_offsets[0] = 0`, `trivial_offsets[1] = 0`, and `off = 0`. So `attr->size = 0`, and the loop `substr_offsets[0] < 0` is false, so no iteration. That's fine.

